{"kind":"report","text":"Independent design review of notes/2026-08-13-second-application.md at exact head 6120d97d416ddd915d22c5ae2886cff6613f6aae. Gate run at that head in its worktree: go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/... — ok, 395.246s, exit 0. The head adds one file over main and changes no behaviour; the verdict is about the design as stated, not about code.\n\nVerdict: CHANGES-REQUESTED. The design is good and most of it should stand. But the trace found one friction budget that is false under the note's own storage choice, one path that cannot be walked as written, one path whose landing statement has no vocabulary to land in, and two invariants the note claims to inherit unchanged but omits. Those are exactly the categories that are not \"record it and move on\".\n\n=== A. FRICTION TRACE, ALL SEVEN PATHS ===\n\n1. SPECTATOR. Steps to first view: 2 (receive URL, open). Prompts 0, installs 0, redirects 0, copy-paste 0. No durable act, so \"steps to first effective act\" is N/A — the honest measure is steps-to-first-view and it is 2. Budget \"no key, no prompt\": HONEST for viewing.\nRemovable: nothing.\nSilent failure (d): the board renders \"from the projection\" with no stated degraded state. Under the chosen forge-primary shape a cold container clones and re-folds at boot, so cold start is the normal case, not the exception; internal/service already has handleRebuild precisely because a cold projection looks like a broken page. A spectator can therefore be shown a stale or partial board with nothing saying so. architecture.md layer 5 is explicit that readers must report an unavailable interpreter rather than present a partial projection as authoritative. The spectator path needs that state named.\nInconsistency: the note promises the nexus \"comes along for free for spectator chat\", but a keyless spectator cannot sign a say. Spectator chat requires a session key, i.e. the anonymous path. Either spectator is keyless and silent, or chat costs the key mint. Say which.\n\n2. ANONYMOUS PLAYER. User actions to first effective act (the join): 2 (open page, tap join). Machine steps: mint Ed25519 via WebCrypto, persist, build payload, hash payload tree, encode canonical intent, sign, POST /v0/submit, re-read projection. Prompts 0, installs 0, redirects 0, copy-paste 0. Budget \"zero prompts before the first move\": HONEST as stated. WebCrypto generateKey needs no permission, so the zero is real.\nRemovable without weakening authority: nothing. I checked the obvious candidate — folding the join tap into the first move — and it must stay: the join is what seats the opponent and the move rests on it, so auto-joining on page open would seat spectators by accident. The path is already minimal. That is a positive finding.\nSilent failures (d), and these are on the primary adoption path:\n (i) The note says the key lives \"WebCrypto Ed25519, non-extractable, in local storage\". A non-extractable CryptoKey cannot go in localStorage — localStorage stores strings only; non-extractable keys are structured-cloneable into IndexedDB and nowhere else. Read literally this is impossible; read loosely it invites the extractable-key-in-localStorage implementation, which any XSS reads. Fix the wording to IndexedDB and keep non-extractable, because the difference is the whole security posture of the path.\n (ii) Script-writable storage is evictable. Safari ITP deletes it after seven days without interaction, and private browsing has none. A correspondence game that spans a week silently loses the player's identity: they return to a board they are seated at and cannot move on, with no explanation the design provides. This is the single worst silent failure in the whole design, and it lands on the zero-prompt path the design cares most about.\n (iii) Non-extractable also means no export, so no second device and no recovery, ever. Not discussed.\n (iv) Join race: two anonymous players tap join, the first effective one seats, the loser's join is \"recorded but judged ineffective\". Whether the loser's page says so is unstated.\n (v) Open submit plus a public projection means every open game is enumerable, so \"the link is the invitation\" is false — a bot can snipe every created game the moment it appears. The note dismisses abuse as \"transport policy... a rate limit\", but a rate limit does not stop a snipe; one request wins. The create vocabulary has no invited-opponent key and no join secret. FINDING (see D2).\n\n3. ANCHORED PLAYER, OIDC. Steps beyond the anonymous path: tap \"log in with GitHub\", redirect out, provider consent screen, redirect back, deployment verifies token and signs the binding. Redirects 1 (round-trip), installs 0, copy-paste 0, prompts 1 (the first-time OAuth consent screen).\nBudget \"one redirect and zero new installs\": HONEST but incomplete. It is carefully not stated as zero prompts, and the consent screen is the step users actually experience as friction, so it should be counted. The deployer-side cost is disclosed honestly elsewhere, which is to the note's credit.\nDECISION-CHANGING CONTRADICTION: the identity section says \"the player's join statement carries that signed binding as evidence\", but this path is defined as \"the anonymous path, THEN log in\" — the player has already joined. The enumerated chess vocabulary is create, join, move, resign, draw-offer, draw-accept. None of them can carry a post-hoc anchor, and there is no attestation statement in the list. So as written, either anchoring must precede the join (which destroys the anonymous-first ordering that is the adoption story), or the vocabulary is missing a statement kind. See D3.\nSilent failure: the redirect navigates away from the game origin and back. Same-origin IndexedDB survives, but if the OIDC callback lands on a different host or subdomain the browser key is simply gone and the returning user is a new actor. Pin the callback to the game origin and say so.\n\n4. ANCHORED PLAYER, USER-HELD KEY. Budget: \"one extension prompt (NIP-07) or one signing-key proof\". This budget is NOT honest, and one of its two variants cannot be walked as written.\n NIP-07 variant: for a user who already runs a NIP-07 extension the real cost is typically TWO prompts, not one — Alby and nos2x prompt separately for getPublicKey and for signEvent on first use. For a user who does not already run Nostr, which is nearly everyone, the honest cost is: install a browser extension from a store, generate or import a key, back up the nsec, then two prompts. The note's OIDC bullet is careful to say \"no extension\", so it knows an extension is required here, yet the budget never counts the install. State the precondition — this budget is honest only for the already-Nostr population, and should say so.\n GitHub-signing-key variant: I CANNOT WALK THIS PATH as written, and by the note's own rule that is a finding. The browser's actor key is minted in-browser and non-extractable; your published GitHub signing key lives in ~/.ssh or on a hardware key. The browser cannot produce a proof relating them. The only real flow is: leave the browser, run ssh-keygen -Y sign at a terminal, copy-paste a signature blob back into the page. That is a terminal round-trip and a copy-paste, not \"one signing-key proof\". Either write those steps down or drop the variant from the browser path and keep it for the CLI population only.\n Separately, this rung's verification is a live fetch of the handle's published keys, which is neither offline nor stable — see D5 on the ladder.\nSilent failure: attestation expiry silently drops the player a rung; nothing says the UI surfaces it.\n\n5. AGENT CREDENTIAL. NO BUDGET STATED — the note says only \"the number of manual steps is the thing to measure\". Walking it: the human must already be anchored (so path 3 or 4 first); generate an agent keypair; sign the attestation over agent key, genesis, scope and expiry with the anchor key (an extension prompt if NIP-07, or a request to the deployment's attestor if OIDC-witnessed); \"hand the agent the key material it needs\"; configure the agent's CLI or MCP adapter with the deployment URL and genesis; then the agent acts. That is roughly six manual steps, one or two prompts, at least one private-key copy-paste, one config edit, and one uncounted install (the agent runtime itself).\nFINDING: the path with the most steps, the only private-key handling by a human, and the highest security sensitivity is the one path carrying no budget. \"The number of manual steps is the thing to measure\" is honest about uncertainty but leaves nothing for a reviewer to check. Give it a budget.\nThe scope-and-expiry attestation is the right mitigation and the strength-inheritance rule (\"only as strong as the anchor that minted it\", and the projection says so) is correct and well stated. Revocation before expiry is a superseding statement, which is right — but it only bites if the fold evaluates the attestation state deterministically, which brings in D6.\n\n6. DEPLOYER. Budget: \"one secret and one command after clone\". THIS BUDGET IS FALSE under the storage shape the note itself chooses, and this is the clearest single reason for changes-requested.\nWalking it: clone the application repository; create the data repository of record on a forge; provision a push credential with WRITE access to that repo so the container can push refs/seq/* (SECOND SECRET); generate the sequencer key; set the sequencer secret (first secret); connect and authorize the platform against the repo; deploy; share the URL.\nForge-primary (R4) means the forge is the repository of record and the container pushes sequence advances to it. Pushing requires a credential the container holds. So the floor is two secrets, not one. The note also states flatly that \"a deployment that skips the OIDC anchor stays at one secret\" — that sentence is wrong for the same reason. Either restate the budget as two secrets, or choose appliance-primary for the recipe and accept that shape's costs instead.\n\n7. SELF-HOSTER. NO BUDGET STATED, and this is also the acceptance path. Walking it: install a Go toolchain (uncounted install) or fetch a release binary; go install the chess module at a tag; make a repo directory; gs init --app \u003curl\u003e@\u003cref\u003e with a 40-hex pinned commit copy-pasted; supply an operator actor name, which gs init requires with no default by deliberate design; a second actor key for the opponent; create; join; move. That is roughly 8-10 steps, 2 installs, and at least one mandatory 40-hex copy-paste.\nCredit where due: a mistyped hash fails LOUDLY — the binding mismatch reports kernel-verifiable but application-uninterpretable, which is the design working exactly as intended. Not a silent failure.\nBut see D1: as written this path also contains a step the design forbids itself from taking.\n\nBudget honesty summary: spectator honest; anonymous honest; OIDC honest but undercounts one consent prompt; user-held-key NOT honest (omits the extension install; one variant unwalkable); agent credential NO budget; deployer FALSE (two secrets, not one); self-hoster NO budget. Two of seven paths carry no budget at all, and both are the CLI paths.\n\n=== B. FINDINGS THAT WOULD CHANGE A DESIGN DECISION ===\n\nD1. gs init --app cannot compute the fold-profile hash under its own rule. The binding is to record \"the application's name, the source URL, the resolved commit hash, and the application's fold-profile hash\", while \"gs init does not fetch, build, or run application code\". Resolving a ref is git ls-remote, which yields a commit hash and nothing else — not the name, not the profile hash. So the profile hash must come from the operator by copy-paste (unverifiable, and it makes the binding a transcription of an operator's belief), or from fetching a manifest (contradicts the rule), or from the binary itself. If it is the binary, then the whole flag collapses: an application ships its own binary that embodies its own identity, so `chess init` already knows its name, commit and profile hash, and `--app \u003curl\u003e@\u003cref\u003e` is redundant on the only path that uses it. Removing it removes a network dependency at init (today init is purely local), a 40-hex copy-paste, and this impossibility, all at once. Decide where the profile hash comes from; I recommend the binary, and dropping --app from the primary path.\n\nD2. Open join has no invitation. Anyone can submit, the projection is public, so open games are enumerable and snipeable; a rate limit does not fix a race that one request wins. The create vocabulary should carry an optional invited-opponent key (or a join secret), or the design should state that open-to-all join is intended and that game creators have no say in who seats.\n\nD3. The OIDC path has no statement to land in. Post-hoc anchoring is required by the path ordering but the enumerated vocabulary has no attestation kind and the identity section puts the evidence on the join. Add the attestation statement to the vocabulary, or change the ordering. As written the two sections contradict.\n\nD4. The one-encoder rule is aimed at the wrong target, and I can show it from the code. internal/intent.Decode re-encodes the decoded intent and refuses unless the bytes are byte-identical (\"intent is not core-deterministic CBOR\"), and internal/kernel reconstructs the payload tree from the submitted bytes and refuses on mismatch (\"payload tree mismatch: signed X, reconstructed Y\"). Both canonicalisations a browser signer must perform are therefore already machine-checked at the kernel boundary with a hard refusal. A second encoder that \"almost agrees\" cannot become a fold divergence — it is dead on arrival, loudly, at submit. So the wasm requirement buys correctness the kernel already provides, while shipping a multi-megabyte Go blob into the one path whose budget the design most wants to protect.\nMeanwhile the genuine two-implementation hazard in web chess is unnamed: the board UI's own legality check versus the Go fold. A JS rules engine that says \"legal\" while the fold judges the move ineffective is silent, is on the anonymous path, and is precisely \"almost agrees\". Re-aim the rule at the rules engine — or better, let the browser never pre-judge legality: propose the move, submit, and reflect the fold's verdict. Then there is only ever one rules engine and the requirement disappears rather than being met.\n\nD5. The anchor ladder collapses two independent axes into one rung. The note ranks OIDC-witnessed below published forge signing key below user-signed Nostr, and says \"the application displays which rung a binding sits on and invents no strength it does not have\". But the rungs differ on two axes, not one. Vouching strength: self-signed beats witnessed, as stated. Verifiability: the Nostr attestation is a signature and is verifiable offline forever; the OIDC binding is a signature by the attestor and is likewise verifiable inside the log; but the forge-signing-key rung is verified by fetching the handle's published keys from a third party at verification time, which is neither offline, nor stable, nor the same answer later — the user can delete the key and retroactively unverify their own history. So the middle rung is the weakest on verifiability while sitting second on strength. Display both facts, or the display does invent strength it does not have.\nCorollary: no fold may perform that fetch. See D6.\n\nD6. Determinism versus expiry and network checks. Chess promises a fold that is \"deterministic and total\", and architecture.md layer 4 makes deterministic interpretation the contract. But the identity design puts attestation verification in the application layer, and attestations carry an expiry, while the forge rung needs a network fetch. Evaluated against wall-clock time or a live endpoint, the fold is neither deterministic nor total, and two readers folding the same log get different projections. This must be pinned: expiry is evaluated against log-internal time — the sequencer-signed timestamp of the position of the statement being judged — never the reader's clock; and no network check may happen inside the fold, which is exactly why the OIDC shape (verify outside, sign the result into the log) is the correctly factored one and the forge rung is not.\n\nD7. The forge-primary shape drops R2's writer lease, and can acknowledge moves that do not survive. The note says it inherits the deployment note's invariants unchanged, but R2 — a per-domain writer lease, a process without it refusing durable writes — is a stated tier-2 BLOCKER and is not mentioned. \"A crashed container is the kernel's own failover story\" covers crash but not overlap, and rolling deploys make overlap the default: two containers alive briefly, both holding the sequencer key. Worse, the topology moves the CAS away from the authority. Each container clones and \"appends locally, and pushes refs/seq/* advances back to the forge\", so each wins its OWN local CAS on a divergent base and only then races the push. The loser's push is rejected non-fast-forward after it has already returned success to a player whose move is now orphaned. The kernel's compare-and-swap protects one repository; it does not protect clone-and-push. Either adopt the R2 lease explicitly, or state the reconciliation rule for a rejected push. This is the most serious correctness finding in the web section.\n\n=== C. THE NAMED DESIGN QUESTIONS ===\n\nQ1, binding in bootstrap versus anywhere, and verification order. Bootstrap prefix is the right call. But the note's own upgrade rule — a successor binding statement mid-log superseding the old one — breaks the positional invariant it just established, and the resulting rule is never stated. State it: a binding is effective only in the bootstrap prefix, or as a supersession of an already-effective binding signed by the initializing key; anything binding-shaped elsewhere is ineffective. Verification order must be fixed explicitly as read-binding-then-select-then-fold, because a host that folds first has already produced state with the wrong interpreter before it checks. Consequence the note misses: because every host must read the binding regardless of which application it implements, the binding CANNOT live in workroom/* schema — it needs a host-level schema family that sits between the kernel and the application profile. architecture.md's layer table has no owner for such a thing today. That is a real contract addition and the implementing head must name it.\n\nQ2, legacy default-to-Workroom versus explicit backfill. Default-to-Workroom is right, and backfill would be a flag day across every existing repository buying nothing, since the absent-binding case is unambiguous — there is exactly one application in existence. Two things to state. First, the authority rule for a legacy repo is defined by reference to a record legacy repos lack (\"the initializing operator's key\" when there is no binding); say that it means the genesis bootstrap operator per the log. Second, and sharper: that authority is NOT revoked by roster retirement, because a host must be able to read the binding without an application, and a chess host has no roster to consult. So a retired Workroom operator's key can still switch a repository's interpreter. I believe the choice is forced — the authority must be host-level, and the only host-level fact is the initializing key — but the asymmetry should be written down rather than discovered later. Note that this grants no new power in practice: that same key already held operator and ratifier from the first event.\n\nQ3, identity-by-commit-hash and the upgrade path. Sound, cheap, and consistent with the content-address discipline used everywhere else; it makes the registry problem genuinely disappear and I would keep it. Two understated costs. The binding should name a FORMAT-QUALIFIED object id, not a bare hash — this repository already writes git:sha1:... everywhere and supports a configurable object format, and an interpreter's identity is a stronger claim to hang on an unqualified SHA-1 than content already covered by signatures. And upgrade is not free: a successor binding changes the fold profile, which invalidates every reader's checkpoint by construction (internal/service says exactly this at handleRebuild), so every host does a full verified re-fold of the whole log. For a long-lived repository that is a real operational event, not just grammar reuse.\n\nQ4, the one-encoder rule for the browser signer. See D4 — the rule as stated solves a problem the kernel already refuses byte-for-byte, at a cost charged directly to the zero-prompt budget, while the actual divergence risk (the browser's rules engine versus the fold) goes unnamed. This is the judgement I feel most strongly about.\n\nQ5, the witnessing actor key and its costs. The shape is right and is the best-factored part of the identity design: the network check happens outside the fold and the durable residue is an ordinary signature by an ordinary actor whose word a reader weighs. The stated costs (attestor key beside the sequencer key, plus a provider registration and client secret) are honestly disclosed. One cost is missing: the attestor's public key must be anchored in the log — the deployment note's R1 uses exactly this pattern, an issuer key anchored in the profile's config log — or historical OIDC bindings become unverifiable the moment the deployment rotates keys or dies, which silently voids the whole rung retroactively. The standing forgery capability (whoever holds the attestor key can mint a binding for any handle) is inherent to witnessing and the note names it correctly; sequencing bounds it, since any minted binding must still be carried by a positioned statement.\n\n=== D. THE THREE CONCLUSIONS ===\n\nARCHITECTURE. Layers touched by the design when implemented: layer 2 (kernel) — correctly unchanged, and chess as a foreign Go module importing the kernel is the honest test the in-tree public_surface_test.go and layout_test.go only gesture at; layer 4 (application profile) — the contract becomes plural and gains a selection rule; layer 5 — internal/app selects one interpreter at open; layer 6 — gs init gains a flag. The compatibility table already reserves a seat for this: the \"application family\" axis is defined as \"schema family, governance bootstrap, and the rules for selecting an interpreter\", so the page anticipated exactly this change. This head is note-only and changes no contract today. The design AS STATED would change the layer-4 contract, and would additionally require a home in the layer table for the host-level binding vocabulary (Q1), which does not exist yet. The note's own step 1 commits the implementing head to updating docs/reference/architecture.md, which satisfies the review rule prospectively. Dependency direction is preserved throughout. No architecture objection to this head.\n\nSECURITY. Boundaries examined: the binding's authority boundary, the anchor ladder, OIDC witnessing, browser key custody, agent credential minting, and the open submit surface. Correctly handled: no private key ever reaches a service (R3 upheld — the browser signer holds its own key, non-extractable); the kernel-verifiable / application-uninterpretable degradation is the honest failure mode and is invoked correctly; strength inheritance for agent credentials is right and the projection is required to say which rung; witnessing invents no new key type; revocation as expiry plus supersession is the right grammar. Unresolved defects, all in the design rather than in this head: browser key custody wording that invites an extractable key in localStorage plus silent eviction (A2 i-iii); fold determinism under expiry and under the forge rung's network check (D6); the ladder conflating vouching strength with verifiability so the displayed rung overstates the middle one (D5); the attestor key not anchored in the log, retroactively voiding OIDC bindings on rotation (Q5); split-brain writes acknowledging orphaned moves under forge-primary with no writer lease (D7); and open join with no invitation (D2). I request changes on D6, D7 and the custody wording in particular.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. A simplification pass already ran, and what it missed is the interesting part — in each case it missed something because the text reads as a single confident sentence.\n (i) The one-encoder rule and its wasm option (D4): a whole browser component, and the heaviest single tax on the design's most protected budget, proposed to prevent a divergence the kernel already refuses. The simplification is to delete the requirement and let the browser propose while the fold disposes, which also deletes the second rules engine.\n (ii) gs init --app (D1): the flag exists to serve the case the design elsewhere says is useless — a generic host binding a foreign application it will not fetch, build, or run. If the application ships its own binary, `chess init` already knows its identity, and dropping the flag removes an impossibility, a network dependency, and a 40-hex copy-paste together.\n (iii) The \"one secret\" claim in the deployer budget (A6) survived the pass because it is stated twice, confidently, and is false both times under the note's own R4 choice.\n (iv) Two of seven paths have no budget, in a section whose entire purpose is budgets.\nNone of these weaken any condition of satisfaction; (i) and (ii) each remove work rather than adding it.\n\n=== E. WHAT I AM NOT OBJECTING TO ===\nThe core decisions are sound and I would ratify them essentially as they stand: one repository one application bound at init; the application as an ordinary Git repository named by a pinned commit; install as interpretation-authority and never execution, with honest degradation; one interpreter selected once at open with no per-event routing; chess in its own repository as the first foreign importer; identity below the application so no application reinvents it; and a minted browser key remaining sufficient to play. Giving up mixed-application repositories and in-place migration is correctly priced. The anonymous path is genuinely minimal and I could find nothing removable from it. My objections are to five places where the stated cost is lower than the real cost, and to two invariants claimed as inherited but omitted.\n\nRecommended shape of the fix: correct the deployer budget to two secrets; state the NIP-07 precondition and either write down or drop the browser-side forge-signing-key variant; give the agent-credential and self-hoster paths budgets; resolve where the fold-profile hash comes from (D1) and drop --app from the primary path; add the attestation statement to the vocabulary or reorder anchoring (D3); pin expiry to log-internal time and bar network checks from the fold (D6); adopt R2's writer lease or state the rejected-push reconciliation rule (D7); fix the key-custody wording to IndexedDB and name the eviction failure; and re-aim or delete the one-encoder rule (D4). None of that changes the spine of the design.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:62062e32b655cb9dad2e2d2345138f4778eb3053","head":"6120d97d416ddd915d22c5ae2886cff6613f6aae","verdict":"changes-requested"}}